title: The Apocrypha
description: Why the Apocrypha is not Scripture: the Old Testament canon Jesus taught, the Septuagint tradition, the Jews and the prophets, the early canon lists, and the errors and heresies of the apocryphal books.
output: lessons/history/the-apocrypha.html

# Built from the notes studies/data/notes/the-apocrypha-notes.html, in the notes' order.
# Every Bible passage is pulled from the KJV by the builder and opens as a pop-up when its reference is tapped.
# The men in the canon timeline open their own words (say: blocks at the end of this file), from public-domain
# translations or translated for this lesson where none exists. The 1 Maccabees lines are quoted as the notes have them
# (underlined words in the notes are bold here).

=== Introduction
--- The Apocrypha [title]
overline: Baptist & church history
heading: The Apocrypha
sub: It is not inspired and is not suitable for doctrine.
refs: 2 Timothy 3:16-17; Luke 24:44
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse, and any name in the canon lists to read what that man wrote.

=== I. The canon Jesus taught
--- The law, the prophets and the psalms
lead: Jesus taught the OT scripture canon: [[Luke 24:44]]
panel: The law | The law is the first 5 books.
panel: The prophets | Prophets are the words of the prophets in the historical books and major and minor prophets.
panel: The psalms | Psalms includes all poetic writing and proverbs.

--- One jot or one tittle
p: Jesus says in [[Matthew 5:18]]:
p: Jots and tittles are explicitly terms from the Hebrew alphabet and written Hebrew. A jot refers to yod, the smallest letter in their alphabet, and tittles a pen stroke or accent mark that differentiates one Hebrew letter from another.
callout: This indicates the audience for what Jesus is teaching is familiar with scripture in Hebrew and would understand His point.

--- In the synagogue
lead: Jesus read from the scriptures in the synagogue, there is no reason to believe it was read in Greek.
xr: Luke 4:14-17 | In the synagogue at Nazareth
xr: Luke 4:18-21 | This day is this scripture fulfilled

--- What Jesus quoted
lead: Jesus quoted the Old Testament 78 times, the Pentateuch alone 26 times.
p: He quoted from Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Deuteronomy, Psalms, Proverbs, Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Daniel, Hosea, Amos, Jonah, Micah, and Malachi. He referred to the Old Testament as “The Scriptures,” “the word of God,” and “the wisdom of God.” The apostles quoted 209 times from the Old Testament.
callout: None of these are quoted from the Apocrypha.

=== II. The Septuagint tradition
--- The Letter of Aristeas
panel-warn: A forgery | The “Letter of Aristeas” that supposedly commissioned “scribes from each of the 12 tribes” to translate the OT into Greek, appears to have been a forgery written by the Alexandrian apostate Jew, Philo, in the first century AD, to justify his heresies.
panel-warn: No 12 tribes | The “12 tribes” did not exist in 250 BC, and there were no scribes in among the long scattered and destroyed 10 tribes of the Northern Kingdom, as all scribes were from the Levites.

--- No evidence
p: There is no evidence of the existence of a Greek translation, other than a copy of Deuteronomy 23-28 in Greek, having zero indication that it was from a larger work, and dated to 150 BC, a century later than the supposed Septuagint. No other evidence outside of Origen exists.
step: ~250 BC | The LXX allegedly dates from 250 BC
step: ~130 BC | and Maccabees was written ~130 BC about events in 170-164 BC, making its inclusion impossible in an LXX based on the tradition of its origin.

=== III. The Jews and the prophets
--- Not of the like authority
lead: The Jews don’t believe the Apocrypha belongs in their Bible, and they never have. [[say:josephus|Josephus]], the greatest Jewish historian of the first century, explained:
quote: “It is true, our history has been written since Artaxerxes very particularly, but has not been esteemed of the like authority with the former by our forefathers.” | Josephus
p: Josephus’ quote is especially helpful here. He indicates that ever since the reign of Artaxerxes (465-424 BC), the Jewish writings (the Apocrypha) have “not been esteemed of the like authority with the former (the Old Testament) by our forefathers.”

--- From Abel unto Zacharias
lead: [[Luke 11:50-51]]
p: Jesus is not only making an A to Z comparison (which is amazing!) but also noting the final prophet, martyred in 2 Chronicles.

--- No prophet among them
lead: Which leads us to Maccabees itself recognizing that no one having the Spirit of God upon them existed in Judea.
quote: So was there a great affliction in Israel, the like **whereof was not since the time that a prophet was not seen among them.** | 1 Maccabees 9:27
quote: Also that the Jews and priests were well pleased that Simon should be their governor and high priest for ever, **until there should arise a faithful prophet** | 1 Maccabees 14:41

--- God spake by the prophets
p: But we know that God spoke through the prophets: [[Hebrews 1:1-2]]
callout: No apocryphal books were written by a prophet. All of the OT Scriptures were written by prophets, while none of the apocryphal books were; therefore, the apocryphal books are not canonical. Scripture attests to this view in that the OT is referred to as the Scriptures of the prophets.

=== IV. The early church canon lists
--- Never considered scripture
lead: Apocrypha was never considered scripture. Not by Jews. Not by the early Christians.
p: Other 2nd–3rd century figures (e.g., Irenaeus, Tertullian, Clement of Alexandria) show familiarity with and occasional citation of some deuterocanonical texts, but none produce a formal canon list that includes them as Scripture equivalent to the protocanonical books.

--- Melito, Origen and Cyril
step: d. 180 AD | [[say:melito|Melito, bishop of the church at Sardis.]] In his work “Extracts” detailing the messianic prophecies about Christ in the Old Testament he provides a “catalogue of the recognized writings of the Old Testament” after which he proceeds to provide the reader with the accepted Old Testament, minus Esther.
step: ca. 220 | [[say:origen|The gnostic Origen,]] in his commentary to Psalm 1, written ca. 220, provides an exhaustive canon of the Old Testament, he, like Melito, excludes the Deuterocanon and presents the canon without Apocrypha. He explicitly places books like Maccabees outside the canon, though he included some deuterocanonical texts in his Hexapla, as uninspired.
step: 313-386 AD | [[say:cyril|Cyril of Jerusalem,]] bishop and patriarch provides his catechumens with the canon, and with a firm warning against the deuterocanonical books.

--- Athanasius, Laodicea and Gregory
step: 367 | [[say:athanasius|Athanasius the Great,]] bishop and patriarch of the church at Alexandria. In his Paschal Letter 39, written 367, Athanasius provides a full presentation of the canonical books of the scriptures.
step: ~360 AD | [[say:laodicea|The Synod of Laodicea.]]
step: 329-380 AD | [[say:gregory|Gregory Nazianzus,]] canon list in his carmina theologica I:I, 12.

--- Also citing the same canon
step: b. ~340 AD | [[say:amphilochius|Amphilochius of Iconium,]] in his letter to Seleucus, line 251-320.
step: 310-403 AD | [[say:epiphanius|Epiphanius of Salamis,]] bishop of the church at Salamis, Cyprus, Against all Heresies, 8.6.1-10.
step: ca. 364 | [[say:hilary|Hilary of Poitiers,]] Instructio Psalmorum 15, written ca. 364.
step: ca. 404 AD | [[say:rufinus|Rufinus of Aquileia]] in his commentary on the apostolic creed.

--- Jerome, Augustine and Trent
step: ca. 380-400 | [[say:jerome|Jerome]] translation into Latin included all of the Deuterocanonical apocrypha, but stated that these books were “not for the establishing of the authority of the doctrines of the church” because they were not inspired.
step: City of God | [[say:augustine|Augustine,]] who stated his version of canon that included the apocrypha in City of God, had a canon that differed from that declared scripture at Trent.
step: 1546 | The Roman Catholic Church officially declared that the Apocrypha was canonical at the **Council of Trent** in 1546, after the Reformation, in reaction to it ([[say:trent|the decree]]), despite Luther and other Protestants including the books, under Apocrypha in their Bible translations.

--- All rejected the Apocrypha
lead: [[say:melito|Melito of Sardis]] · [[say:origen|Origen]] · [[say:hilary|Hilary of Poitiers]] · [[say:athanasius|Athanasius of Alexandria]] · [[say:cyril|Cyril of Jerusalem]] · [[say:gregory|Gregory of Nazianzus]] · [[say:epiphanius|Epiphanius of Salamis]] · [[say:amphilochius|Amphilochius of Iconium]]
p: All compiled lists of OT books, and all rejected the Apocrypha as being inspired. (Because they are obviously not.)
callout: No doctrine comes from church fathers. It comes from scripture, through the Holy Spirit.

=== V. Errors and heresy
--- Theological and historical errors
lead: The Apocrypha contains theological and historical errors.
panel-warn: Wisdom 11:17 | For example, the Book of Wisdom indicates that God created the world out of preexisting matter (11:17) which contradicts the rest of Scripture’s teaching that God created the world out of nothing.
panel-warn: Judith 1:5 | Moreover, the book of Judith incorrectly states Nebuchadnezzar was king of Assyria, when in fact, he was the king of Babylon (1:5).
panel-warn: Tobit | The book of Tobit also claims that the author was alive when Israel revolted from Judah and when the tribe of Naphtali was taken to Nineveh, which would mean he lived to be at least 216 years old.

--- Heresy
panel-warn: Judith 9:10, 13 | Heresy includes Judith 9:10, 13, which claims that God helped Judith in a deed of falsehood, painting God as a liar.
panel-warn: Ecclesiasticus and Wisdom | Ecclesiasticus and Wisdom teach morality based on expedience rather than the word of God.
panel-warn: Tobit (early 2nd century BC) | Tobit is a short novel that emphasizes the Law, clean foods, ceremonial washings, fasting and prayer for righteousness. It is clearly unscriptural in its statement that alms giving atones for sin.

--- The Wisdom of Solomon
panel-warn: The Wisdom of Solomon (about 180 BC) | The Wisdom of Solomon was written by an Alexandrian Jew who impersonated as Solomon. In it, the gnostic/Platonic concept of the preexistence of the soul before birth is taught, when the Bible makes it clear we are created at conception.
panel-warn: Tobit 6:5-7 | Condones the use of magic in Tobit 6:5-7.

--- Purgatory and 2 Maccabees 12
callout: II Maccabees 12:39-46 is cited by the Romans for the damnable heresy of purgatory. That Jesus isn’t sufficient to cleanse a believer of sins, but that they must themselves atone for “unrepentant sin.” yet it does not explicitly teach this;

--- Killed by God for idolatry
p: First, the passage acknowledges that the men were killed by God for idolatry (v. 40). Thus, they died in their idolatry, and Scripture says this of idolaters: [[1 Corinthians 6:9-10]]
p: And as it says in [[Hebrews 9:27-28]];

=== Close
--- The Apocrypha
lead: Apocrypha contains false doctrines such as prayer for the dead, false cures, witchcraft, virtue in burning the heart of a fish to drive devils away, alms deeds delivering from death and sin, and salvation by works.
- It has factual errors in contrast to the OT scripture.
- It never claims to be the word of God, admits it is not written by prophets, in the Spirit.
callout: It is not inspired and is not suitable for doctrine.

